  "narrative": {
    "intro": "HUBZone Set-Aside Federal Contracts data for FY2026 to date show $437M in contract obligations across 1,303 awards, with an average award size of $336K. Obligations fell 52.5% year over year, reflecting a lower fiscal-year-to-date total compared with prior years. Obligations by fiscal year were $938M in FY21, $1B in FY22, $1.1B in FY23, $1B in FY24, and $920M in FY25, before the FY26 to date total of $437M. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $151M in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $77.1M and the Department of Labor with $53.4M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Transportation with $30.7M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $28.2M. Other Computer Related Services is the largest industry by obligations at $118M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $42.7M and Other Technical and Trade Schools at $41.7M. Top vendors in FY2026 to date include T&H SERVICES, LLC with $22.8M and ANACAPA MICRO PRODUCTS, INC. with $19.8M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2026 to date compare with prior fiscal years?",
        "body": "Fiscal-year obligations were $938M in FY21, $1B in FY22, $1.1B in FY23, $1B in FY24, and $920M in FY25. FY26 to date obligations are $437M, down 52.5% year over year. The award count in FY26 to date is 1,303 with an average award size of $336K. These figures are fiscal year to date and current-year totals are partial."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries lead HUBZone set-aside obligations in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense leads with $151M in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $77.1M and the Department of Labor with $53.4M. The largest industry by obligations is Other Computer Related Services at $118M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $42.7M and Other Technical and Trade Schools at $41.7M. Top vendors include T&H SERVICES, LLC with $22.8M and ANACAPA MICRO PRODUCTS, INC. with $19.8M."
      }
    ]
  },
